<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khirbat en-Nahas</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Khirbet en-Nahas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Khirbat en-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naha">Nahas</a>, 'ruins of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>', is a large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> copper-smelting site in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faynan">Faynan</a> district of southern Jordan. Excavations led by Thomas Levy and Mohammad Najjar from 2002 dated much of its activity to the 10th and 9th centuries BCE, earlier than once believed, and linked it to the rise of an Edomite elite. The site has a fortress, buildings and huge slag mounds.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khirbat-en-nahas.jpg" alt="Khirbat en-Nahas" width="320" height="303" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Robert Simmon · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Khirbat_en-Nahas.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
